%
%
%

\section{Homomorphisms and \texorpdfstring{$(\cup, \times)$-}{Union-Product }Circuits}
         \label{s:circuit}
        %
In this section we define $\{\cup, \times\}$-circuits and explain how they can be used to represent sets of homomorphisms. We roughly follow the presentation of Amarilli and Capelli \cite{DBLP:journals/sigmod/AmarilliC24}; although we use the language of `sets of functions' as opposed to relations, see also Remark~\ref{rem:fan_in}.

Let $(X,D)$ be a pair of finite sets. Then a \emph{generalised $(\times, \cup)$-circuit over $(X,D)$}
%
 is a vertex-labelled directed acyclic graph with a unique sink such that every internal (i.e.\! non-source) node has fan-in two and is labelled by either $\times$ or $\cup$ and every source is labelled with an expression of the form $x \mapsto d$, where $x \in X$ and $d \in D$. We call nodes labelled by $\circ \in \{\cup, \times\}$ $\circ$-gates and sources input-gates or simply inputs to the circuit. We call the set $X$ the \emph{variables} and the set $D$ the \emph{domain} of the circuit. 

 For $\C$
%
 a $\{\cup, \times\}$-circuit over $(X,D)$ and $g$ a node of $\C$, we say that a variable $x \in X$ \emph{occurs below} $g$ if there is some $d \in D$ and input $g'$ to $\C$ labelled with $x \mapsto d$, such that there is a directed path from $g'$ to $g$ in $\C$. We write $\var(g)$ for the set of variables occurring below $g$ and $\var(\C)$ for the set of all variables occurring in the circuit. With these notions in hand we can now define the main representation format studied in this paper. 

\begin{definition}[$\{\cup, \times\}$-circuit]
A $\{\cup, \times\}$-circuit over $(X,D)$ is a generalised $\{\cup, \times\}$-circuit $\C$ over $(X,D)$ with $\var(\C) =X$ such that
\begin{enumerate}
\item for every $\times$-gate $g$ in $\C$ with children $g_1, g_2$ we have $\var(g_1) \cap \var(g_2) = \emptyset$, and
\item for every $\cup$-gate $g$ in $\C$ with children $g_1, g_2$ we have $\var(g_1) = \var(g_2)$.
\end{enumerate} 
\end{definition}

