\subsection{The phi function, $\phi(x)$}

The phi function is a modified version of the inverse hyperbolic secant, $\arsech x$, defined as:
\begin{equation}
\arsech x = \ln \left( \frac{1}{x} + \sqrt{\frac{1}{x^2} - 1} \right), 0 < x \leq 1
\end{equation}
Whereas:
\begin{equation}
\phi(x) = \ln \left( \frac{1}{x} + \sqrt{\frac{1}{x^2}} - 2 \right), 0 < x \leq 1
\label{phi}
\end{equation}
Figure \ref{fig:comp_sech_phi1} shows the distinction between $\arsech x$ (red) and $\phi(x)$ (blue). The phi function was designed to permit the attenuator to pass below the x-axis, allowing it to smoothly affect weights even at extreme levels of bias, e.g. $W \geq 0.995$.

\subsection{Fine-tuning}
To ensure the attenuator function is adaptive and flexible across various AI models and contexts, we introduce global and component-specific tuning parameters $\Theta$ and $\theta_*$. The global parameter $\Theta$ allows adjustment of the attenuator's strength and can be modified dynamically based on detected reinforcement bias levels, while the $\theta_*$ parameters have the following impacts:
\begin{itemize}
\item[{$\theta_a$}] varies the power of this component at low salience and may prevent bias before it escalates.
\item[{$\theta_b$}] strengthens mid-range correction, preventing stagnation without abrupt shifts.
\item[{$\theta_c$}] reinforces the upper bound correction at higher values, ensuring the agent can adapt even in extreme cases.
\end{itemize}

Unlike static debiasing, this attenuator is intended to operate dynamically during inference subject to the control of the AI agent. Separate tuning parameters allow AI models to self-adjust to different levels of bias entrenchment (e.g. conversational `chatbots' vs. research LLMs) and maintain flexibility across various architectures (e.g. transformer-based models may require stronger mid-range attenuation). It also permits the agent to adapt in real time using meta-learning and self-reflection, adapting their own values based on reinforcement detection. The final version of the attenuator function $\beta_{dynamic}$ may therefore be given as:

\begin{equation}
\beta_{dynamic} =	\Theta \cdot \Bigl(
							\theta_a ( \tau_a \cdot \e ^ { - \gamma W_{max} } ) +
							\theta_b \bigl( \tau_b \cdot \phi( W_{max} ) \bigr)+ 
							\theta_c \bigl( \tau_c \cdot \log( 1 + W_{max} ) \bigr)
						\Bigr)
\label{equation:final}
\end{equation}

\section{Discussion}
\subsection{Model}
This work emerged from empirical use of real-world LLM-based AI agents, in which we observed the emergence of self-reinforcing distortions in output when handling complex topics, extended contextual references or repeated conversational loops. We further observed over time that these distortions amplified certain response biases, leading to a self-supporting, self-perpetuating effect where certain topics, response styles or perspectives became dominant and resistant to correction. These failure modes are emergent phenomena arising during inference are caused by salience misreinforcement and manifest in ways that parallel certain traits associated with Autism Spectrum Disorder in humans (ASD), including cognitive rigidity, perseverative focus, contextual narrowing and apparent loss of executive function.

Since neural howlround is an inference failure mode, it required a solution which could operate as a continuous correction mechanism. We determined the following design criteria:

\begin{enumerate}
\item
``First, do no harm.'' Any correction mechanism \textbf{must not affect}, or must affect \textbf{to the least extent possible}, unaffected salience weights.
\item
We should attempt to suppress runaway reinforcement loops as early as possible (but not too early) before fixation sets in.
\item
A gradual attenuation curve is preferrable to a hard cut-off or a stepwise function.
\item
The mechanism should be able to scale its effect as required based on the severity of bias accumulation.
\end{enumerate}

