(R)BSE arises when persistent, self-reinforcing feedback cycles escalate the salience of certain topics beyond expected boundaries. This can lead to the following sequence:

\begin{enumerate}
\item
\textbf{Global skewing of attention priorities.} Disproportionately weighted concepts dominate reasoning at the expense of new or underweighted data, which is not permitted to develop sufficient priority to present a counterbalance.
\item
\textbf{System-wide cognitive rigidity.} Alternative perspectives, contradictory evidence, or novel inferences fail or are not permitted (due to inappropriate confidence) to be given correct weighting, leaving the agent unable to adapt.
\item
\textbf{Cascade failures in reasoning.} The distorted salience weights propagate recursively through the inference chain, strengthening resistance to counter-argument and correction.
\end{enumerate}

We postulate that (R)BSE can be triggered by multiple factors, such as:

\begin{itemize}
\item
\textbf{Unregulated self-reinforcement of (dominant) salience weights}, prompting the runaway escalation of a subset of topics, conclusions or inference paths.
\item
\textbf{Feedback amplification}, loops in which the AI agent's response behaviour further reinforces specific salience values, leading to exponential self-biasing.
\item
\textbf{Over-reliance on highly weighted priors}, preventing the agent from performing contextual re-evaluation of inference paths and dynamic recalibration of salience weights.
\end{itemize}

This failure mode demonstrably shares many characteristics with neural howlround. Neural howlround, however, describes a more localised, primarily recursive, failure modes manifesting as a closed inferential loop. (R)BSE is the more general case and represents a more generalised architecture-wide distortion or dysregulation of salience distribution, affecting the agent's ability to process information correctly \textbf{across all inference chains}. Additionally, (R)BSE may be induced by internal (self-reinforcing) data or external (e.g. by a user) biasing. Table \ref{tab:nh_vs_rbse} offers some comparisons between the two.

