\section{Methods}
\label{sec:Approach}
\subsection{ReVeal Framework}
We propose ReVeal, a multi-turn reinforcement learning (RL) framework that enables code agents to engage in an iterative generation-verification loop. This framework leverages the model's reasoning capabilities, using a single model not only to generate candidate solutions, but also to construct executable verification plans and revise its outputs based on structured feedback from external tools. In the code generation context, \textit{generation} refers to producing code solutions, while \textit{verification} involves generating test cases to uncover potential errors and evaluating code correctness based on their execution results. Through repeated iteration between generation and verification, the model progressively improves its accuracy and reliability across multiple reasoning turns.

\subsubsection{Iterative Generation-Verification Loop}
As illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig: framwork}, each iteration in ReVeal consists of two stages: generation and verification. In the generation stage, the LLM produces a candidate code solution. In the verification stage, it constructs test cases and invokes external tools (i.e., Python interpreters) to execute the code and verify its correctness. The resulting tool feedback is then appended to the previous rollouts and incorporated into the prompt for the next turn. This process repeats until the generated code passes verification or a maximum number of turns is reached.

Table~\ref{case_study} presents an example of a multi-turn rollout. Instead of relying on the commonly used <think>-<answer> prompting~\citep{guo2025deepseek}, ReVeal adopts a more structured prompting format that explicitly decouples generation, verification, and tool feedback into an iterative loop using distinct tags. 
As shown in the example, the model first reasons about the problem under <generation-think> and produces a candidate solution within <generation-answer>. It then initiates the verification phase, constructing a plan under <verification-think> by analyzing potential failure modes, edge conditions, and the intended behavior of the code. These test cases, either derived from the problem description or newly synthesized to expose likely errors, are specified under <verification-answer> for direct execution. The <tool-feedback> section captures execution results, including runtime errors, invalid test cases, as well as the expected output, actual output, and pass/fail judgment for each valid test case. This structured feedback provides fine-grained supervision and guidance for the next generation-verification cycle. Based on this feedback, the model identifies failed cases, diagnoses underlying errors, and revises both code solutions and verification plans accordingly. This process continues over multiple turns, allowing the model to progressively refine its outputs through next rounds of generation and verification - enabling self-improvement without requiring external critic models or predefined test cases. The prompt and feedback template are listed in Table~\ref{Prompt_Table} and Table~\ref{feedback_mapping}.

