\subsubsection{Tool-Augmented Verification}
The interaction between the LLMs and external tools plays a critical role in ensuring the accuracy and reliability of the verification process. During training, execution results such as runtime errors, defective test cases and failed test cases are parsed into structured <tool-feedback>. This feedback guides the model's next generation-verification turn, enabling it to revise both the candidate code and its associated test cases. Importantly, such execution feedback provides highly informative signals that guide the model to explore a broader and more promising solution space beyond its original exploration boundary. This enhanced exploration capability is especially valuable in RL, where discovering higher-quality positive examples can significantly improve policy learning.

To ensure feedback quality during training, we adopt a filtering mechanism: test cases generated by the model are executed on the candidate code only if they are verified as correct against a golden solution. This ensures that execution traces reflect legitimate supervision, thereby improving the precision of the feedback and guiding exploration toward correct solutions. At test time, however, golden code is not available. All generated test cases are assumed as valid, making the verification process fully autonomous. This imposes a strong demand on the model's ability to generate high-quality test cases. To support this requirement, we develop a customized training algorithm that significantly enhances the model's capability to plan and generate high-quality test cases, enabling reliable self-verification during inference.

Finally, during RL training, the <tool-feedback> section is excluded from the loss computation and treated solely as contextual input. This masking strategy stabilizes training and preserves the model's coherent reasoning sequences from disruption.

\subsection{Multi-Turn RL Algorithm for the Generation-Verification Paradigm}
% \subsubsection{Preliminaries}
\subsubsection{Turn-level Reward Design}
While outcome-only rewards in RL have shown promise in enabling LLMs to learn self-verification, we ask: \textit{is the current paradigm fully leveraging this capability? Can denser, higher-quality rewards lead to more effective training?} To address these questions, we propose a  structured set of turn-level, verifiable rewards for both generation and verification turns. 
Our extensive experiments demonstrate that, when guided by dense turn-level supervision, the generation-verification paradigm not only effectively optimizes both generation and verification capabilities but also enables effective test-time scaling, supporting continuous performance improvement beyond the original training regime.

To guide the model in generating both code and a corresponding verification plans (i.e., test cases), we decompose the final reward into an outcome reward and dense per-turn rewards for both generation and verification. The outcome reward consists of a format reward \(r_{\mathrm{format}}\) and a pass-rate reward \(r_{\mathrm{passrate}}\): 
\begin{equation}
r_{\mathrm{outcome}}
= r_{\mathrm{format}} + r_{\mathrm{passrate}}.
\end{equation}
We employ a format reward to ensure that the model generates iterative generation and verification tags in the correct order, and wraps both code and test cases in the required format for easy extraction:
\begin{equation}
r_{\mathrm{format}}
=
\begin{cases}
1, & \text{if the output format is correct},\\
-1, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
$r_{\mathrm{passrate}}$ represents the fraction of ground-truth test cases passed by the final code, serving as a measure of code accuracy:
\begin{equation}
r_{\mathrm{passrate}}
= 5 \times \mathit{passrate},
\quad
\mathit{passrate} \in [0,1],
\end{equation}
so that \(r_{\mathrm{passrate}}\in[0,5]\) and \(r_{\mathrm{outcome}}\in[-1,6]\).

We use $k$ to denote the turn index in the generation-verification loop, where odd $k$ corresponds to generation turns and even $k$ to verification turns. For each generation turn \(k\), we first compute the pass-rate \(r_{\mathrm{passrate}}^k\) for the code generated in that turn, and then define the per-turn generation reward as:
\[
r_{\mathrm{gen}}^k =
\begin{cases}
r_{\mathrm{passrate}}^1, & k = 1,\\[6pt]
\mathit{abs}\cdot r_{\mathrm{passrate}}^k + \mathit{imp}\cdot\bigl(r_{\mathrm{passrate}}^k - r_{\mathrm{passrate}}^{k-2}\bigr), & k \ge 3,
\end{cases}
\]
where \(\mathit{abs}\) and \(\mathit{imp}\) are hyperparameters that weight the absolute code accuracy and the improvement of code accuracy over the previous turn respectively. In our experiments, we set \(\mathit{abs}=0\) and \(\mathit{imp}=1\).

For each verification turn \(k\), we let
\begin{equation}
r_{\mathrm{ver}}^k
= \frac{\#\{\text{test cases in turn }k\text{ that pass}\}}
       {\#\{\text{test cases generated in turn }k\}},
\end{equation}
i.e.,\ the proportion of generated test cases that succeed when executed on the golden code.

\subsubsection{Training Algorithm}
\paragraph{Preliminaries}
Our algorithm builds on the Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) framework~\citep{schulman2017proximalpolicyoptimizationalgorithms}, an on-policy actor-critic algorithm that optimizes a clipped surrogate
 objective to ensure stable policy updates. PPO typically computes advantage estimation using Generalized Advantage Estimation (GAE)~\citep{schulman2018highdimensionalcontinuouscontrolusing}:
\begin{equation}
\hat{A}_t^{\mathrm{GAE}(\gamma,\lambda)} 
= \sum_{l=0}^{\infty} (\gamma\lambda)^l \bigl(r_{t+l} + \gamma V_{t+l+1} - V_{t+l}\bigr),
\end{equation}
where $\gamma\in[0,1]$ is the discount factor and $\lambda\in[0,1]$ controls the bias-variance trade-off.  

\paragraph{Turn-Aware PPO (TA-PPO)}
Building on our structured turn-level reward design, we introduce Turn-Aware PPO (TA-PPO), which preserves the standard PPO actor-critic framework but replaces the conventional GAE-based advantages with a \emph{turn-aware} return. TA-PPO leverages the critic to efficiently bootstrap over both token-level Monte Carlo returns and turn-level returns, enabling stable learning across these two reward scales.

1. Token-level return. We set $\lambda = 1$ (pure Monte Carlo) and $\gamma = 1$. Let $T$ denote the final token step and $r_t$ the token-level reward. This yields the undiscounted cumulative return at token step $t$:
\begin{equation}
R_t \;=\; \sum_{l=0}^{T-t} r_{t+l}
       \;=\; r_t + R_{t+1}, 
\quad
R_{T+1} = 0.
\end{equation}
2. Turn-level return. To mitigate adversarial reward gaming (e.g., generating trivial code that hacks the verification reward), we introduce a \emph{turn-level} return tailored to the generation-verification interplay. Specifically, (i) each generation reward is assigned both to its own generation turn and to the immediately preceding verification turn, and (ii) each verification reward is confined strictly to its own verification turn. This design prevents reward hacking by ensuring that generation turns are rewarded solely based on code quality, rather than verification success. Let $\{t_1, \dots, t_K\}$ denote the token indices at which each turn ends  (alternating generation and verification), and define
\begin{equation}
R^{\mathrm{turn}}(t_k) =
\begin{cases}
r_{\mathrm{gen}}^k, 
& \text{if turn }k\text{ is generation},\\[4pt]
r_{\mathrm{ver}}^k + R^{\mathrm{turn}}(t_{k+1}), 
& \text{if turn }k\text{ is verification},
\end{cases}
\quad
R^{\mathrm{turn}}(t_{K+1}) = 0.
\end{equation}
For an arbitrary token at step \(t\), we define 
\[
\tau(t) = \min\{t_k \mid t_k \ge t\},
\]
and set
\begin{equation}
R^{\mathrm{turn}}_t =
\begin{cases}
R^{\mathrm{turn}}\bigl(\tau(t)\bigr), & \text{if } \tau(t)\text{ exists},\\
0, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Finally, we combine the standard Monte Carlo return with the turn-level return to construct a turn-aware return:
\begin{equation}
\widetilde R_t = R_t + R^{\mathrm{turn}}_t,
\qquad
A_t = \widetilde R_t - V_t,
\end{equation}
where \(V_t\) is the critic model's estimate at step \(t\). These advantages $A_t$ then replace the standard GAE estimates in the PPO objective, completing the TA-PPO update.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:Experiments}
\subsection{Settings}
\paragraph{Dataset} We construct our training dataset from TACO \citep{li2023tacotopicsalgorithmiccode}, a large-scale corpus comprising  26,443 algorithmic programming problems sourced from competitive programming platforms such as LeetCode \citep{leetcode} and Codeforces \citep{codeforces}. Each problem consists of a natural language description, golden solutions, and multiple test cases.

To address noise in the raw dataset, we first filter out problems containing unsupported content types, specifically those tagged with interactive or image elements. To ensure testability and correctness, we process two types of test case format, function-based tests and standard input/output tests, into a unified structure compatible with our code execution environment. We then execute each test case against the first available golden solution in our execution environment. Problems where the golden code fails to pass all associated test cases are discarded. After preprocessing, we retain a high-quality dataset of 11,151 problems for training and 509 problems for testing.

\paragraph{Models and Training Details}
We use Qwen2.5-32B-Instruct~\citep{qwen2025qwen25technicalreport} as our main backbone. However, when applying RL to this model using only code-related data, we did not observe the spontaneous emergence of long chain-of-thought capabilities with reflection, consistent with the findings of SRPO~\citep{zhang2025srpocrossdomainimplementationlargescale}. To address this, we adopt DAPO-Qwen-32B~\citep{yu2025dapoopensourcellmreinforcement} as our base model, which is reinforced with mathematical data, and we continue RL training on code datasets to adapt its reasoning capabilities to coding tasks. Our models are trained using the Verl~\citep{sheng2024hybridflow} framework on 8/16 AMD Mi300x GPUs. The RL training process follows the hyperparameter settings listed in Table \ref{RL_Training_Hyperparameters}.We set the maximum turns to 3 during RL training.

\paragraph{Evaluation} We evaluate our approach on the LiveCodeBench (2024.08-2025.01) benchmark \citep{jain2024livecodebenchholisticcontaminationfree}, a holistic and contamination-free benchmark for code LLMs. The evaluation process follows the hyperparameter configuration specified in Table \ref{RL_Evaluation_Hyperparameters}. Although training is performed with a maximum of 3 turns, we evaluate the model under extended turn settings (6 and 19 turns) to assess its generalization to longer reasoning horizons and test-time scaling performance.

We use Pass@1 to measure the success rate of the model's final code solutions. To evaluate the model's verification and self-correction capabilities, we introduce two additional metrics: $\Delta_{\uparrow}$ denotes the fraction of initially incorrect solutions that become correct after revision, and $\Delta_{\downarrow}$ denotes the fraction of initially correct solutions that become incorrect after revision. In line with recent work~\citep{yue2025doesreinforcementlearningreally}, which uses Pass@k metrics to assess whether RL can push the reasoning boundaries beyond the base model, we also evaluate Pass@k for values of k up to 128, using a maximum of 6 turns per example.

\paragraph{Code Execution Tool} We use Code Judge\footnote{\url{https://github.com/0xWJ/code-judge}} as our code execution environment for training, evaluation, and data preprocessing. Code Judge supports both function-based and standard input-output test case formats through a consistent interface. Designed for scalability and robustness, it enables efficient long-batch execution through multi-processing and provides reliable code evaluation.
\paragraph{Baselines}
We compare ReVeal against the following baselines: (1) \textit{Base}: the base models without code-specific RL training; (2) \textit{CTRL~\citep{xie2025teachinglanguagemodelscritique} + Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct}: a dedicated code critic model (CTRL) and Qwen2.5-Coder-Instruct policy alternate critique and revision for five turns (results taken from the original paper); (3) \textit{Single-turn RL with outcome reward}: RL with outcome-only rewards under the standard <think>-<answer> prompting template without any external tool calls; (4) \textit{DeepSeek-R1-Zero-Qwen-32B}: a strong RL-zero baseline trained on both math and code data without any supervised fine-tuning.

