\begin{abstract}

Compilers play a foundational role in building reliable software systems, and bugs within them can lead to catastrophic consequences. The compilation process typically involves hundreds of files, making traditional automated bug isolation techniques inapplicable due to scalability or effectiveness issues. Current mainstream compiler bug localization techniques have limitations in test program mutation and resource consumption.
%Current mainstream methods transform the bug isolation problem into a test program mutation problem. By mutating a given failing test program, a set of passing test programs (i.e., witness test programs) is generated. Then, the coverage information of both passing and failing test programs is collected, and suspicious files are ranked using Spectrum-Based Fault Localization (SBFL) techniques. Existing compiler bug Isolation techniques can be categorized into two types: one locates bugs by mutating failing test cases(such as DiWi, RecBi, and LLM4CBI), and another locates bugs by changing optimization options and sequences (such as ODFL and LocSeq). DiWi, RecBi, and LLM4CBI have limitations in generating diverse test programs and in utilizing available information. For example, DiWi only supports local mutations, such as changing the type of a variable. RecBi supports more structural mutations (i.e., changing the control flow of the program), but it can only generate conditional statements without statement bodies. LLM4CBI uses LLMs to generate compiler programs, but it does not utilize other useful information to guide LLMs in locating bugs.ODFL focuses on isolation issues by disabling individual optimization options, mainly targeting bugs related to a single optimization option. However, it may not be effective in locating complex bugs involving multiple optimization options. On the other hand, LocSeq focuses on interaction issues between optimization sequences, using genetic algorithms to find alternative optimization sequences for bug localization. However, it has significant limitations in terms of search space and resource consumption.
Inspired by the recent advances of pre-trained Large Language Models (LLMs), %in the fields of information retrieval and reasoning, 
we propose an innovative approach named \tool{}, which (1) uses LLMs to summarize compiler file functions and (2) employs specialized prompts to guide LLM %\hy{how many LLMs are used? Across the entire paper (in both texts and Figure 2), the term "LLMs" is used, should be it a/the LLM if only one LLM is used?}] 
in reordering suspicious file rankings. This approach leverages four types of information: 
the failing test program, source file function summaries, lists of suspicious files identified through analyzing test coverage, as well as compilation configurations with related output messages, resulting in a refined ranking of suspicious files.
% the suspicious file ranking list generated by traditional methods, the compiler file function summaries, the test cases that trigger the bug along with their output results under different optimization options, and the coverage differences between failing and passing test programs. 
% This results in a new ranking of suspicious files.
Our evaluation of \tool{} against state-of-the-art approaches (DiWi, RecBi and FuseFL) on 120 real-world bugs from the widely-used GCC and LLVM compilers demonstrates its effectiveness. Specifically, \tool{} isolates 66.67\%/69.23\%, 300\%/340\%, and 100\%/57.14\% more bugs than RecBi, DiWi, and FuseFL, respectively, in the Top-1 ranked results for GCC/LLVM. Additionally, the ablation study underscores the significance of each component in our approach.
% Compared with the state-of-the-art approaches (DiWi, RecBi and FuseFL) over 120 real-world bugs from the two most popular compilers, namely GCC and LLVM, our evaluation demonstrates the effectiveness of \tool{}: it can isolate 66.67\%/69.23\% , 300\%/340\% and 100\%/57.14\% more bugs than RecBi, DiWi and FuseFL, respectively, over GCC/LLVM within Top-1 ranked results. The ablation study highlights the importants of each component in our approach.
\end{abstract}
