\subsection{Contributions}
The contributions of this work are summarized as follows: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item 
We propose novel diffusion learning strategies to solve stochastic two-network game problems and analyze their convergence.
Our work relaxes the strong assumption of a bipartite graph without isolated nodes for the cross-team graph structure, as considered in previous studies (e.g. \cite{gharesifard2013distributed, lou2015nash, huang2024no}). Unlike existing methods, our algorithm employs a constant step size, enabling agents to continuously adapt toward an equilibrium state.
Furthermore, simulation results illustrate that the proposed methods achieve faster convergence and offer an improved stability range for the step size compared to the baseline \cite{vlaski2021competing}.

    % \item We consider algorithm design for two types of cross-network connections: weak and strong. These concepts capture minimal and dense connectivity patterns between competing networks (see Definition \ref{def:cross-network}). Inspired by \cite{ying2016information, salami2017social}, we show that a well-designed diffusion learning strategy can achieve convergence even with weak cross-network connections in the zero-sum case. For more general two-network problem, we propose two tailored diffusion learning algorithms for both weak and strong cross-network connections, incorporating an additional assumption on agents' available information in the weak connection scenario (see Assumption \ref{ass:add_grad}).
    % {\cblue Our network structure considered in this work has been weakened compared to the existing works. Apart from the bipartite network structure, we also propose algorithms for addressing ..... We also consider left stochastic matrix for agents that share the same goal while those works use dooubly stochastic.}

    % \item We employ a {\cblue non-decaying} step size for all agents, facilitating continuous adaptation and learning. This choice allows our algorithm to handle dynamic environments with shifting Nash equilibria, unlike methods that rely on decaying step sizes.

    \item 
  Our algorithm is simple and efficient to implement, as it requires only left-stochastic matrices for within-team communication, avoiding the additional step of constructing right-stochastic matrices \cite{zimmermann2021solving, pang2023distributed, zhou2022distributed, zhu2025accelerated, wang2025gradient, yu2024distributed}. Moreover, we do not impose convexity on local objective functions and instead assume that the global gradient operator satisfies strong monotonicity.

    \item 
We apply our algorithms to important practical applications, including the Cournot team competition in economics and decentralized GAN training. Simulation results confirm that our proposed algorithms outperform existing baselines.


\end{enumerate}
