The MATLAB functions, \texttt{ssefun} and \texttt{hessmfun}, do not require the RTToolbox. However, they exploit efficiencies derived using the \ac{RT} algebra. Figures~\ref{fig:ssefun} and~\ref{fig:hessmfun} clarify the (inverse) 2D \acp{DFT} revealed after algebraic manipulations that involved purely numeric aspects of tensor operands, some nonzero degree. The dual-variant index notation excelled on a variety of inner, entrywise, outer, and mixed products.

Where one of two operands is multipage, a 3D array, the \ac{HMF}, \texttt{hessmfun}, leverages \ac{EMV} broadcasting. An operator, \texttt{.*}, enables entrywise products with pagewise broadcasting. The \ac{HMF} uses pagewise kernel routines, \texttt{fft2} and \texttt{ifft2}, also. Thus, the example motivates nonscalar and broadcasting extensions of Ricci notation featured by the \ac{RT} algebra.

