      \field{labeltitlesource}{title}
      \field{abstract}{We consider a model of quantum computation in which the set of elementary operations is limited to Clifford unitaries, the creation of the state \${|}0{\textbackslash}rangle\$ computational basis. In addition, we allow the creation of a one-qubit ancilla in a mixed state \${\textbackslash}rho\$, which should be regarded as a parameter of the model. Our goal is to determine for which \${\textbackslash}rho\$ universal quantum computation ({UQC}) can be efficiently simulated. To answer this question, we construct purification protocols that consume several copies of \${\textbackslash}rho\$ and produce a single output qubit with higher polarization. The protocols allow one to increase the polarization only along certain "magic" directions. If the polarization of \${\textbackslash}rho\$ along a magic direction exceeds a threshold value (about 65\%), the purification asymptotically yields a pure state, which we call a magic state. We show that the Clifford group operations combined with magic states preparation are sufficient for {UQC}. The connection of our results with the Gottesman-Knill theorem is discussed.}
