      \field{abstract}{We refine ideas from Knill 1996, Jones 2016, Chamberland 2020, Gidney 2023+2024, Bombin 2024, and Hirano 2024 to efficiently prepare good \${|}T{\textbackslash}rangle\$ states. We call our construction "magic state cultivation" because it gradually grows the size and reliability of one state. Cultivation fits inside a surface code patch and uses roughly the same number of physical gates as a lattice surgery {CNOT} gate of equivalent reliability. We estimate the infidelity of cultivation (from injection to idling at distance 15) using a mix of state vector simulation, stabilizer simulation, error enumeration, and Monte Carlo sampling. Compared to prior work, cultivation uses an order of magnitude fewer qubit-rounds to reach logical error rates as low as \$2 {\textbackslash}cdot 10{\textasciicircum}\{-9\}\$ when subjected to \$10{\textasciicircum}\{-3\}\$ uniform depolarizing circuit noise. Halving the circuit noise to \$5 {\textbackslash}cdot 10{\textasciicircum}\{-4\}\$ improves the achievable logical error rate to \$4 {\textbackslash}cdot 10{\textasciicircum}\{-11\}\$. Cultivation's efficiency and strong response to improvements in physical noise suggest that further magic state distillation may never be needed in practice.}
