The opaque nature of the SVD obfuscates placement of bounds on both the required $r$ and on the unitary gates needed to implement $V,W$ in terms of $H$, making it difficult to extend the implementation of postselection to non-universal cases. However, when $H$ is either diagonalizable non-$PT$-Symmetric, or single qubit $PT$-symmetric, we can directly solve for the gates required to implement postselection, which makes the proof more transparent and thus more generalizable. In general, the SVD can have exponential cost to calculate, but this can be treated as a constant for fixed $m$ since it need only be performed once. Moreover, it can often be reduced, since we only need the SVD in the subspace corresponding to $\lambda_1,\lambda_d$.

\subsection{Diagonalizable, non-$PT$-Symmetric}

If $H$ is diagonalizable and not $PT$-Symmetric, i.e., has a complex spectrum, the time evolution operator generates exponential growth or decay of eigenspaces without the need for interspersed unitaries.
An example of such Hamiltonians includes the NH transverse field Ising model (TFIM)~\cite{barch2023scrambling, gopalakrishnan_entanglement_2021} for some $\gamma > g$ or the Hamiltonian given in \cref{eq:H-pt-sym} below, for $\gamma>h$. 

Consider the spectral decomposition 
\begin{align}
    H = \sum_j (h_j+i\gamma_j) \ketb{r_j}{l_j}\quad\gamma_1\leq\gamma_2\leq...\leq\gamma_d
\end{align}
for $h_j,\gamma_j\in\mathbb{R}$. Assume that the imaginary part of the spectrum obeys $\gamma_d-\gamma_1\ge 1/\poly(n)$, or else $H$ is exponentially close to being $PT$-Symmetric (up to a constant shift, which disappears under normalized evolution). Then the time evolution operator
\begin{align}
    U = \sum_j e^{-\gamma_j t}e^{-i h_j t}\ketb{r_j}{l_j}
\end{align}
decays or grows the amplitude in each right-eigenspace $\ket{r_j}$ at rate $\gamma_j$. 

As shown in \cref{app:deorthogonalize}, we can explicitly find unitaries to implement postselection in the relevant subspace, $\text{span}\{\ket{r_1},\ket{r_d}\}$. Though the $\{\ket{r_j}\}$ right eigenbasis is not orthogonal, we can pick unitaries $\hat V, \hat W$ (not necessarily the same as in the SVD) such that

\begin{align}
\label{eq:diag-decay}
\begin{split}
     \hat D &= \hat V^\dag U \hat W\\
     &= a_r a_l e^{-\gamma_1 t} \ketbra{0^m} + e^{-\gamma_d t}G+\dots\\
    &\sim \ketbra{0^m} + e^{(\gamma_1-\gamma_d) t} a_r^{-1} a_l^{-1} G+\dots
    \end{split}
\end{align}
where $a_r,a_l$ are constants and $G$ a constant matrix on the subspace spanned by $\ket{0^m},\ket{1,0^{m-1}}$, and we can ignore the rest of the gate as long as ancillas are initialized to $\ket{0^{m-1}}$ as in the previous subsection. The second and third lines are equivalent under normalized evolution. 
As $\gamma_d>\gamma_1$, the $e^{(\gamma_1-\gamma_d)t}$ term will decay exponentially in $t$, and $\hat D$ will become an approximate projector onto $\ket{0^m}$ in the $\ket{0^m},\ket{1,0^{m-1}}$ subspace, approximating postselection. Unlike in the general SVD construction, a single application of $H$ for time $t \ge c m/(\gamma_d-\gamma_1)$, with constant $c\ge 1$, is sufficient, which is efficient when $\gamma_d-\gamma_1\ge 1/\poly(n)$. 

\subsection{Single Qubit $PT$-Symmetric}
\label{sec:ptsym}
In the case of single qubit $PT$-Symmetric $H$, it is possible to directly calculate the singular values $\lambda_1,\lambda_2$ and necessary unitaries $V,W$ in terms of $H$. As shown in \cref{app:arbitrary}, under a proper choice of orthonormal basis and neglect of terms proportional to the identity, any single qubit $PT$-symmetric Hamiltonian can be written up to a constant shift as
\begin{align}
\label{eq:H-pt-sym}
H = \left(\begin{matrix}
        0 & h-\gamma\\
        h+\gamma & 0
    \end{matrix}\right) ,
\end{align}
for $0 < \gamma < h$. Additionally, the associated time evolution operator is
\begin{align}
    U = \text{cos}(\omega t)\ident - \frac{i}{\omega}\text{sin}(\omega t) H ,
\end{align}
where $\omega = \sqrt{h^2-\gamma^2}\in\mathbb{R}$. Notably, when $t = \frac{\pi}{2\omega}$, $U = -i\omega^{-1} H$. Let $\sigma^x$ be the bit flip operator in this basis, and $g_{\pm} = (h\pm\gamma)/\omega$. Then
\begin{align}
    \begin{split}
        (\sigma^x U)^r &= 
        \begin{pmatrix}
  g_+^{r} & 0\\
  0         & g_-^{r}
\end{pmatrix} ,
    \end{split}
\end{align}
up to a phase, so we find $\lambda_1=g_+, \lambda_2=g_-, W=\ident$, and $V=\sigma^x$. In this case, we can see that the only unitary gate necessary to implement postselection is the bit flip in the current basis (universal unitaries are still needed for the surrounding BQP computation), which is in fact proportional to $H_0 = h\sigma^x$, the Hermitian part of $H$. 

To suppress the $\ket{1}$ component to error $\varepsilon = 2^{-\poly(n)}$ it suffices to choose
\beq
   r = \Bigl\lceil\frac{\log(1/\varepsilon)}{\log\bigl(g_{+}/g_{-}\bigr)}\Bigr\rceil
   =
   O\left(\poly(n) \frac{h}{\gamma}\right).
\eeq
Thus $r=\poly(n)$ provided $\gamma/h \ge 1/\poly(n)$.


\section{Simulability}
\label{sec:simulability}

