Unfortunately, \cref{thm:NH-universality} establishes a lower bound on worst-case hardness only when the NH gate set together with arbitrary unitaries already forms a universal family.
The present proof does not address restricted NH models (e.g., fixed geometry or symmetries) that fail to generate universality.
It is difficult to make broad claims about the computational complexity of such systems, but we can place an upper bound on the complexity of a subset by a simulability proof. In particular, we give non-unitary extension of Clifford, matchgate, and strongly simulable tensor network circuits, that remain strongly simulable.

To show this, we consider the purification of NH dynamics into unitary evolution combined with postselection. When the unitary system is strongly simulable, i.e., has conditional output probabilities calculable in P to exponentially small additive error~\cite{bremner2011classical, nest2009classical, johnson2013solving}, this then acts as a framework for strongly simulating the NH system as well. We consider circuits incorporating channels of the form shown in \cref{fig:non-unitary-decomposition} acting on system $A$ and a meter (i.e., ancilla) $B$. Applying this circuit to some system state $\rho$ with meter output postselected onto $\ket{s}$ gives the output state $\mathcal{E}_s(\rho) = \tilde{\mathcal{E}}_s(\rho)/\Tr [\tilde{\mathcal{E}}_s(\rho)]$ for the non-normalized channel
\begin{align}
    \tilde{\mathcal{E}}_s(\rho) &\equiv \bra{s} U \left(\rho\otimes\ketb{\phi}{\phi}\right) U^\dag \ket{s}_B = C_s\rho C_s^\dag ,
\end{align}
and a non-unitary effective circuit $C_s \equiv \bra{s} U \ket{\phi}_B$, with $\ket{\phi}_B \equiv \ident\otimes\ket{\phi}$.

In instances where $U$ acting on $\rho\otimes\ketb{\phi}{\phi}$ is strongly simulable, we can compute $P_U(x | s)$ for strings $x,s$, and thus can calculate
\begin{align}
    P_{\mathcal{E}_s}(x) &= \bra{x}\mathcal{E}_s(\rho)\ket{x}
    = \frac{P_U(x,s)}{P_U(s)}= P_U(x|s) ,
\end{align}
where 
\begin{align}
P_U(x,s) = \bra{x}\tilde{\mathcal{E}}_s(\rho)\ket{x} = \bra{x}C_s\rho C_s^\dag\ket{x} ,
\end{align} 
and $P_U(s) = \sum_x P_U(x,s)$. One can see that the conditional probability $P_{\mathcal{E}_s}(x_1|x_2) = P_U(x_1|x_2, s)$ is similarly calculable.
This inherits efficiency, hence establishing strong simulability of $\mathcal{E}_s$ on $\rho$, provided the success probability $P_U(s)$ is at least $2^{-\poly(n)}$.

As there is no universal framework for determining system simulability, we consider non-unitary extensions of paradigmatic strongly simulable unitary systems: Clifford and matchgate circuits, and tensor network dynamics.  The two main derivations of NH systems, as no-jump trajectories~\cite{ashida2020nonhermitian, ashida2018full} or as generalizing Hermiticity to $PT$-symmetry~\cite{mostafazadeh_pseudo-hermiticity_2002, karuvade2022observing}, motivate different methods of purifying NH dynamics, which we cover separately.


\subsection{Clifford Circuits}
As a simple example, we can consider a non-unitary extension of the paradigmatic Clifford circuits. Clifford circuits are a subset of quantum circuits generated by the gates $W, S$ and CNOT, where $W$ is the Hadamard and $S$ a $\frac{\pi}{4}$ rotation about the $Z$ axis. Clifford circuits are defined as normalizing the Pauli group, i.e., $U\sigma U^\dag = \sigma'$ for any Clifford circuit $U$ and Pauli strings $\sigma$ and $\sigma'$. Per the Gottesman-Knill theorem, evolution which begins in a computational basis state, applies a Clifford circuit, and ends in a computational basis measurement, can be efficiently strongly simulated~\cite{gottesman1998heisenberg, jozsa2013classical}. 

As a building block of non-unitary Clifford circuits, we can think of the $n$-qubit subcircuit $C$ generated by applying an $(n+1)$ qubit Clifford circuit $U$ to $n$ system qubits plus a one-qubit meter, which is both initialized and postselectively measured into the $\ket{0}$ state. That is, take the circuit in \cref{fig:non-unitary-decomposition} with $m=1$ and $\ket{\phi}=\ket{s}=\ket{0}$ so that
\begin{align}
\label{eq:post-cliff}
    C \equiv \expval{0}{U}_B
\end{align}
While $C$ can be included in a Clifford circuit without breaking simulability, it itself is not Clifford. Applying $C$ to an $n$-qubit Pauli string $\sigma$ yields
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
    C\sigma C^\dag &= \expval{0}{U (\sigma\otimes\ketb{0}{0}) U^\dag}_B\\
    &= \frac{1}{2}\expval{0}{U [\sigma\otimes(\ident+\sigma^z)] U^\dag}_B\\
    &= \frac{1}{2}\left(\expval{0}{\sigma_1}_B+\expval{0}{\sigma_2}_B\right)
    \end{split}
\end{align}
for $(n+1)$-qubit Pauli strings $\sigma_1 = U (\sigma\otimes\ident)U^\dag$ and $\sigma_2 = U(\sigma\otimes \sigma^z)U^\dag$. Each $\expval{0}{\sigma_k}_B$ is an $n$-qubit Pauli string if the $(n+1)$ term in $\sigma_k$ is $\ident$ or $\sigma^z$ and $0$ if it is $\sigma^x$ or $\sigma^y$, so $C$ maps a single Pauli string to a linear combination of zero to two Pauli strings. This has the potential to lead to a number of Pauli strings growing exponentially in the number of non-unitary subcircuits, making the overall circuit inefficient to simulate directly (i.e., without ancillas) under techniques which track the state of a fixed number of Pauli strings. Nonetheless, the circuit is still simulable via inclusion of the postselected ancillas using standard stabilizer-tableau measurement updates~\cite{anders:022334}, highlighting that adding non-Clifford gates to the Clifford group does not necessarily yield a quantum advantage when the underlying circuit is non-unitary.

As a simple case, consider $U = \text{CNOT}_{12}$, the CNOT gate with the ancilla qubit as the target. The effective circuit is $C = \ketb{0}{0}$, the projector onto the $\ket{0}$ state. A class of circuits generated by Clifford subcircuits and such projectors was previously studied in Ref.~\cite{sang2023ultrafrast}, where it was shown to demonstrate a range of unique properties, including ultrafast spreading of conditional mutual information.

Interestingly, the non-Clifford subcircuit nature does not occur if postselection is replaced with a trace. Consider the circuit of the form of \cref{fig:non-unitary-decomposition} where postselection is replaced by a trace, so that $\mathcal{E}(\ketbra{\psi}) = \Tr_B[U(\ketbra{\psi}\otimes\ketb{0}{0}) U^\dag]$. If we consider the expectation $\<\sigma\>$ of some $n$-qubit Pauli string, we find
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
    \<\sigma\> &= \Tr [\sigma \Tr_B[U(\rho\otimes\ketb{0}{0}) U^\dag]]\\
    &= \Tr [\sigma\otimes \ident_BU(\rho\otimes\ketb{0}{0}) U^\dag]\\
    &= \Tr [\expval{0}{\sigma_1}_B\rho] .
\end{split}
\end{align}
As $\expval{0}{\sigma_1}_B$ is at most one Pauli string, the unconditional open circuit does not increase the number of Pauli strings on which a state $\rho$ is supported, and so need not increase the hardness of simulability under Pauli-string based simulations.

\subsection{Matchgate Circuits}

Matchgate circuits are another strongly simulable class of circuits~\cite{valiant, jozsa2008matchgates, shtanko2021complexity, projansky2024extending}. They are composed of two-qubit gates of the form
\begin{align}
    U(F,G) = \left(\begin{matrix}
        F_{11} & 0 & 0 & F_{12}\\
        0 & G_{11} & G_{12} & 0\\
        0 & G_{21} & G_{22} & 0\\
        F_{21} & 0 & 0 & F_{22}\\
    \end{matrix}\right)
\end{align}
with $\det(F) = \det(G)$. When these gates act on nearest neighbor pairs of a 1D lattice, the circuit is strongly simulable for product state inputs via mapping to free fermions using the Jordan-Wigner transformation. 

Consider applying a gate $U(F,G)$ on a system qubit and ancilla, where the ancilla is initialized into the $\ket{+}$ state and postselectively measured into the $\ket{0}$ state. Then the resulting effective gate on the system qubit is
\begin{align}
    \bra{0}U(F,G)\ket{+}_B = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left(\begin{matrix}
        F_{11} & F_{12}\\
        G_{21} & G_{22}
    \end{matrix}\right)
\end{align}
which is equivalent to an arbitrary single qubit gate under normalized evolution, since the remaining $F_{ij},G_{ij}$ can be chosen to satisfy unitarity, and set $\det(F)=\det(G)$. Alternatively, picking the ancilla to begin in $\ket{0}$ ($\ket{1}$) makes the resulting gate a linear combination of $\ident$ and $\sigma^z$ ($\sigma^x$ and $\sigma^y$) without the $1/\sqrt{2}$ factor.

The 1D restriction limits where ancillas can be added without affecting the nature of the circuit, restricting us to two total non-unitary gates, one on each of the first and last qubits. As a result, the effective circuit is at most a constant factor more complicated to simulate directly. In a sense, this is necessary: were we able to apply arbitrary single-qubit gates to any qubit, the model would become universal for BQP.  


\subsection{Trotterized NH Dynamics}

The method of Trotterization used for simulating Lindblad evolution~\cite{cleve2017efficient, ding2024simulating} can be extended to simulations of NH systems, by treating it as a discrete-timestep version of the no-jump trajectory derivation in Ref.~\cite{ashida2020nonhermitian}. This method works by alternating unitary dynamics on a system-meter pair with measurement on the meter. When measurement results are traced over, the resulting evolution is unconditional, and the result is a method of simulating Lindbladian dynamics~\cite{cleve2017efficient, ding2024simulating}. However, when the measurements have outcomes postselected as in \cref{fig:non-unitary-decomposition}, the resulting evolution is conditional on those outcomes and can reproduce an arbitrary quantum trajectory. In particular, postselecting on the meter's initial state (i.e., $\ket{s}=\ket{\phi}$) each step yields the no-jump trajectory, and effective evolution under an NH system Hamiltonian. 

The derivation here is an extension of those in Refs.~\cite{ashida2020nonhermitian, cleve2017efficient} in that it applies to simulations of arbitrary trajectories and accounts for the locality necessary for tensor network simulations. We focus on the no-jump trajectory in this section, but a general derivation for arbitrary trajectories and Lindblad dynamics is included in \cref{app:trajectories-purified}. 


Consider a Hamiltonian $H = H_A + \frac{1}{\sqrt{\delta}}H_{AB}$ acting on system $A$ and meter $B$, where for some basis $\{\ket{j}\}$ of the meter
\begin{align}
\label{eq:H-meter}
    H_A = \sum_j H_j\otimes\ketb{j}{j}\ , \quad H_{AB} = \sum_{j\neq k} L_{jk}\otimes\ketb{j}{k} ,
\end{align}
with $L_{jk}=L_{kj}^\dag$ so that $H_{AB}^\dag=H_{AB}$. While $H_A$ is not truly local on $A$, for short timesteps $\delta$ with the meter initialized to a basis state $\ket{j}$ it will \emph{effectively} be local to $O(\delta)$. For such a timestep, we have 
\begin{align}
\label{eq:U-timestep}
\begin{split}
    U &= e^{-iH\delta}\\
    &= \ident-i\sqrt{\delta} H_{AB}-i\delta H_A-\frac{\delta}{2}H_{AB}^2+O(\delta^{3/2}) .
    \end{split}
\end{align}
Notice that terms with non-integer powers of $\delta$ map $\ket{j}\rightarrow\ket{k\neq j}$, i.e., are off-block-diagonal in the meter basis. Then, if we initialize and measure the meter to be in some state, e.g., $\ket{0}$ as in \cref{fig:non-unitary-timestep}, the effective non-unitary system timestep is

\begin{align}
\begin{split}
    C &= \expval{0}{U}_B\\
    &= \ident_A-i\delta \expval{0}{H_A}_B-\frac{\delta}{2}\expval{0}{H_{AB}^2}_B+O(\delta^{2})\\
    &= \ident_A-i\delta\left(H_0-\frac{i}{2}\sum_j L_{j0}^\dag L_{j0} \right) + O(\delta^2)\\
    &= e^{-i\delta H^0_\text{eff}} + O(\delta^2) ,
\end{split}
\end{align}
where the terms with non-integer powers of $\delta$ disappear, and $L_{0j}=L_{j0}^\dag$. This gives a timestep of evolution under the usual no-jump Hamiltonian
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
    H^0_\text{eff} &= H_0 -\frac{i}{2}\sum_j L_{j0}^\dag L_{j0}\\
    &= \expval{0}{H_A}_B-\frac{i}{2}\expval{0}{H_{AB}^2}_B.
\end{split}
\end{align}
$H^0_\text{eff}$ can be an arbitrary NH Hamiltonian up to an inconsequential (under normalized evolution) overall imaginary shift, since the anti-Hermitian part $\sum_j L_{j0}^\dag L_{j0} \geq 0$ but is otherwise free. It is an open question to investigate whether the Trotter error can be reduced to higher orders, e.g., via the techniques of Refs.~\cite{ding2024simulating, cleve2017efficient}, as methods for Trotterized unitary evolution~\cite{childs2021theory} do not extend as naturally to the case where unitary evolution is alternated with a projector.

