\section{Mapping Orthogonal to non-Orthogonal States}
\label{app:deorthogonalize}
Here we show how to pick $\hat V^\dag, \hat W$ to diagonalize non-$PT$-symmetric $H$ to the extent necessary to implement postselection [\cref{eq:diag-decay}]. A generally diagonalizable $U$ takes the form
\begin{align}
    U = \sum_j e^{-(ih_j+\gamma_j)t}\ketb{r_j}{l_j}\quad\gamma_1\leq\gamma_2\leq...\leq\gamma_d .
\end{align}
Non-orthogonality of $\ket{r_1},\ket{r_d}$ means we cannot unitarily rotate both $\ket{0^m}$ onto $\ket{r_1}$ and $\ket{1,0^{m-1}}$ onto $\ket{r_d}$. However, it is sufficient to instead rotate $\ket{0^m}$ onto $\ket{r_1^\perp}$, the component of $\ket{r_1}$ orthogonal to $\ket{r_d}$, which can be done unitarily. That is, let 
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
    \ket{\tilde r^\perp_1} &= \ket{r_1} - \frac{\brak{r_d}{r_1}}{\bk{r_d}{r_d}}\ket{r_d}\\
    \ket{r_1^\perp} &= \frac{\ket{\tilde r^\perp_1}}{\| |\tilde r^\perp_1\>\|} ,
    \end{split} 
\end{align}
and define the unitary
\begin{align}
    \hat W= e^{ih_1t}\ketb{r^\perp_1}{0^m}+e^{ih_dt}\ketb{r_d}{1,0^{m-1}}+\dots
\end{align} 

After Gram–Schmidt orthogonalization produces the two orthonormal vectors $\ket{r^\perp_1}$ and $\ket{r_d}$, we can adjoin any orthonormal basis of the orthogonal complement to obtain a full $2^m\times 2^m$ unitary $\hat{W}$. The explicit choice of that complement does not affect the two-dimensional block relevant for postselection, so we leave it unspecified.

Then by using biorthonormality $\bk{l_i}{r_j}=\delta_{ij}$, we find that
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
    U\hat W &= a_r e^{-\gamma_1 t}\ketb{r_1}{0^m} \\
    &+ e^{-\gamma_d t}\ket{r_d}\left(\bra{1,0^{m-1}}+b_r \bra{0^m}\right)+\dots
\end{split}
\end{align}
for $a_r = \bk{l_1}{r^\perp_1}=\||\tilde r^\perp_1\>\|^{-1}$ and $b_r = e^{i(h_1-h_d)t}\bk{l_d}{r^\perp_1} = e^{i(h_1-h_d)t}a_r \frac{\brak{r_d}{r_1}}{\bk{r_d}{r_d}}$.
We can similarly orthogonalize $\ket{l_1},\ket{l_d}$ by defining $|\tilde l_1\> = \ket{l_1}-\frac{\brak{l_d}{l_1}}{\bk{l_d}{l_d}}\ket{l_d}$ and $\ket{l_1^\perp} =|\tilde l_1^\perp\>/\||\tilde l_1^\perp\>\|$. Similarly to $\hat W$, we can define $\hat V^\dag = \ketb{0^m}{l^\perp_1}+\ketb{1,0^{m-1}}{l_d}+\dots$ and obtain 
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
    \hat V^\dag U \hat W &= a_r a_l e^{-\gamma_1 t} \ketbra{0^m} + e^{-\gamma_d t}G+\dots    \end{split}
\end{align}
where in the $\ket{0^m},\ket{1,0^{m-1}}$ basis the matrix $G$ is
\begin{align}
    G = \left(\begin{matrix}
        b_r b_l & b_l\\
        b_r & 1
    \end{matrix}\right)
\end{align} 
for $a_l,b_l$ defined analogously to $a_r,b_r$ on the corresponding left eigenvectors.

\section{Single Qubit $PT$-Symmetric $H$}
\label{app:arbitrary}
\subsection{$PT$-Symmetric $H$}
Here we show how to approximate postselection with an arbitrary single qubit $PT$-Symmetric $H$. First, decompose $H = H_0 -i\Gamma$ for Hermitian $H_0, \Gamma$, and write $H_0 = h\vec n\cdot \vec\sigma$ and $\Gamma = \gamma\vec m\cdot\vec\sigma$ for unit vectors $\vec n, \vec m$, where $\vec\sigma$ is the Pauli vector. Notice that $\vec n \neq \vec m$ or else $H = (h-i\gamma)\vec n\cdot\vec \sigma$ has complex eigenvalues and cannot be $PT$-Symmetric. Gram-Schmidt lets us extend $\vec n, \vec m$ to an orthonormal basis of $\mathbb{R}^3$, $\{\vec v_i\}_{i=1}^3$ where $\vec v_1 = \vec n$ and $\vec m = m_1\vec v_1+m_2\vec v_2$ for some $m_1,m_2$. With $\sigma_i = \vec v_i\cdot\vec\sigma$ for convenience, we can write $H_0 = h\sigma_1$ and $\Gamma = \gamma(m_1\sigma_1 + m_2\sigma_2)$.

For $H$ to be $PT$-Symmetric there must exist some $\eta > 0$ such that $H^\dag\eta = \eta H$. Represent $\eta = r_0\ident + \vec r\cdot\vec\sigma$. Writing out $H^\dag\eta - \eta H = 0$ in terms of coefficients of $H_0, \Gamma, \eta$ using commutation and anticommutation properties of the Pauli group yields
\begin{align}
    \begin{split}
        0 &= H^\dag\eta - \eta H = [H_0,\eta] +i\{\Gamma, \eta\}\\
        &= 2h(\vec n \times \vec r)\cdot\vec\sigma +2i\gamma(r_0 \vec m\cdot\vec\sigma + \vec m\cdot\vec r \, \ident) .
    \end{split}
\end{align}
This yields four equations, corresponding to the three Pauli operators and the identity, respectively:
\bes
\begin{align}
        2i\gamma r_0 m_1 &= 0\\
        -2h r_3 + 2i\gamma r_0 m_2 &= 0\\
        2h r_2 &= 0\\
        m_1 r_1 + m_2 r_2 &= 0 .
\end{align}
\ees
As $h,\gamma\neq0$ by necessity, we must have $r_2=0$. Taking $m_1\neq0$ makes $r_0,r_1,r_3$ and thus $\eta$ zero, so we must have $m_1=0$. Then $m_2=1$, and so $r_3 = r_0\frac{h}{\gamma}$ while $r_0$ and $r_1$ are free. In other words, we can write
\begin{align}
    \begin{split}
        H &= h\sigma_1 -i\gamma\sigma_2\\
        \eta &= r_0(\ident + \frac{\gamma}{h}\sigma_3) + r_1\sigma_1
    \end{split}
\end{align}
Finally, $\eta>0$ implies $r_0,r_1\in\mathbb{R}$ and
\begin{align}
    \sqrt{\frac{\gamma^2}{h^2}+\frac{r_1^2}{r_0^2}}<1
\end{align}
which can only be satisfied when $\gamma<h$. Written in the basis of $\sigma_3$, $H$ becomes entirely real valued:
\begin{align}
    H = \left(\begin{tabular}{c c}
        $0$&$h-\gamma$\\
        $h+\gamma$&$0$
    \end{tabular}\right) .
\end{align}
Thus, we see that the toy model studied in Ref.~\cite{gopalakrishnan_entanglement_2021}, which is in fact a single qubit case of the imaginary TFIM~\cite{gopalakrishnan_entanglement_2021, matsumoto_continuous_2020, barch2023scrambling}, is actually universal for single-qubit $PT$-symmetric systems.

\subsection{Ellipsoidal Periodic $U$}
Thanks to the anticommutation of $\sigma_1,\sigma_2$, $H^2 = (h^2-\gamma^2)\ident$ cleanly, so the Taylor series of $e^{-iHt}$ decomposes into a cos and sin term like in the Hermitian case. With $\omega^2 = h^2-\gamma^2$,
\begin{align}
    U = \text{cos}(\omega t)\ident -\frac{i}{\omega}\text{sin}(\omega t)H .
\end{align}
Intuitively, this periodic behavior makes sense as a quasi-Hermitian Hamiltonian generates rotations on the ellipsoid defined by metric $\eta$. It is interesting to note that the exceptional point at $h=\gamma$ manifests as $\omega\rightarrow0$, signaling the breakdown of periodicity.

\section{Distance from Unitarity}
\label{app:unitary-distance}
For $U=e^{-iHt}$ with SVD $U=VDW^\dag$, the effective non-unitary operator applied in each step is
\beq
  D  = \sum_{j=1}^{d}\lambda_j \ketb{s_j}{s_j},
  \qquad
  \lambda_1  >  \lambda_d \ge  0 .
\eeq
Define the \emph{normalized singular radius}
\beq
\label{eq:Delta}
  \Delta \equiv 1 - \frac{\lambda_d}{\lambda_1} \in (0,1] .
\eeq

\subsection{Singular Radius and the Minimum Distance from Unitarity}

One can show that $\Delta$ is related to the minimal distance between any normalization choice $\alpha U$ of $U$ and the unitary group $\mathcal{U}(d)$ as
\begin{align}
    \Delta = \frac{1}{c}\min_{\alpha,T}\Vert \alpha U - T\Vert
\end{align}
for $\alpha>0$, $T\in\mathcal{U}(d)$, and some $c\in(\frac{1}{2},1]$. To see this, first notice that the minimizing unitary will be the polar factor $VW^\dag$, as 
\begin{align}
    \Vert \alpha U - VW^\dag\Vert\leq\Vert\alpha U-T\Vert
\end{align} for all unitary $T$ \cite{fan1955some}. Then we can minimize over $\alpha$:
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
    \min_\alpha \Vert \alpha U &- V W^\dag\Vert = \min_\alpha \Vert \alpha D-\ident\Vert\\
    &= \min_\alpha \max_j(\vert \alpha \lambda_j-1\vert)\\
    &= \min_\alpha \max(\vert\alpha\lambda_1-1\vert,\vert \alpha\lambda_d-1\vert)\\
    &= \frac{\lambda_1-\lambda_d}{\lambda_1+\lambda_d}\\
    &= \frac{\lambda_1}{\lambda_1+\lambda_d}\Delta
\end{split}
\end{align}
for minimizing $\alpha = 2/(\lambda_1+\lambda_d)$, and $c=\lambda_1/(\lambda_1+\lambda_d)\in(\frac{1}{2},1]$. 

Alternatively if one considers the distance between un-normalized $U$ and the group of operators proportional to unitaries, $\mathbb{R}^+\cdot\mathcal{U}(d)$, one finds
\begin{align}
    \min_{\alpha,T}\Vert U - \alpha T\Vert = \frac{\lambda_1}{2}\Delta.
\end{align}
This distance is similar but slightly harder to interpret since it depends on $\Vert U\Vert=\lambda_1$, which is non-physical under normalized evolution.

\subsection{Singular Radius Bound}
\label{app:gap}

After $r$ repetitions of the effective non-unitary gate $D$ as in \cref{sec:NHBQP}, the Kraus operator is $D^r$, whose singular values are
$\lambda_j^r$.  
To emulate postselection in the subspace $\text{span}\{\ket{s_1},\ket{s_d}\}$, we choose $r$ large enough that the renormalized map suppresses the $j=d$ branch below an error
$\varepsilon = 2^{-p(n)}$ for polynomial $p(n)$, i.e., 
\begin{align}
\label{eq:lambda_err_bound}
    \bigl(\lambda_d/\lambda_1\bigr)^r  \le  \varepsilon = 2^{-p(n)}.
\end{align}
Let $r_\text{min}$ be the minimum (not necessarily integer) $r$ satisfying~\cref{eq:lambda_err_bound}. Then
\beq
  r_\text{min} = \frac{\ln(1/\varepsilon)}{\ln(\lambda_1/\lambda_d)}
   \in \Theta \Bigl(\frac{p(n)}{-\ln(1-\Delta)} \Bigr),
\eeq
since $\lambda_1/\lambda_d = (1-\Delta)^{-1}$ [\cref{eq:Delta}]. As $0<\Delta\le 1$, we can bound $-\ln(1-\Delta) \ge \Delta$ so that 
\beq
r_\text{min}\in O\left(\frac{p(n)}{\Delta}\right)\ .
\eeq
Additionally, when $\Delta\le 1/2$ we also have, by Taylor expansion, $-\ln(1-\Delta)=\Delta +\frac{1}{2}\Delta^2+\cdots \le 2\Delta$ so that $r_\text{min}\in\Omega(p(n)/\Delta)$ as well. In both cases picking $r\in\Theta(p(n)/\Delta)$ is \emph{sufficient} to postselect with error $\varepsilon$, while for small $\Delta$ it is also \emph{necessary}.


Thus,
\begin{itemize}
  \item If $\Delta  \ge  1/\poly(n)$, one can pick $r\in O(\poly(n))$, and the construction is efficient.
  \item If $\Delta  \le  2^{-\poly(n)}$, then $r \in \Omega \bigl(p(n)2^{\poly(n)}\bigr)$ is necessary --- exponential and inefficient, breaking the complexity result.
\end{itemize}

Hence, we must assume that $\Delta$ is at least inverse-polynomial in $n$. Equivalently, the implementation of postselection in \cref{sec:NHBQP} is inefficient if and only if some normalization of $U$ is super-polynomially close [i.e., closer than $1/\poly(n)$] to being a unitary.

\section{Trajectories Derivation}
\label{app:trajectories-purified}

Here we show how the Hamiltonian in \cref{eq:H-meter} generates quantum jumps and Lindblad evolution, in addition to the previously covered no-jump trajectories. Recall that we have a timestep unitary $U$ defined via \cref{eq:U-timestep},
acting on system $A$ and meter $B$.
 To obtain a quantum jump from the trajectory under $H_\text{eff}^j$ to that of $H_\text{eff}^k$, we apply a Trotter step with the meter initialized into the $\ket{j}$ state and postselected into the $\ket{k}$ state. Then after timestep $\delta$, the state of some initial system state $\rho$ is proportional to
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
    \rho(\delta) &\sim \tilde{\mathcal{E}}_k(\rho) = \expval{k}{U\left(\rho\otimes\ketbra{j}\right)U^\dag}_B\\
    &= \delta \expval{k}{H_{AB}\left(\rho\otimes\ketbra{j}\right)H_{AB}}_B\\
    &= \delta L_{kj}\rho L_{kj}^\dag + O(\delta^{3/2}) ,
\end{split}
\end{align}
where we used $L_{kj}^\dag = L_{jk}$ and the fact that $I$ and $H_A$ both preserve the meter state $\ket{j}$. This gives the form in \cref{eq:jump}. The scaling $H_{AB}/\sqrt{\delta}$ we used in \cref{eq:U-timestep} ensures that (i) a jump occurs with probability $O(\delta)$ at each Trotter step, and  
(ii) conditioned on no jump, the meter’s back-action reproduces the non-Hermitian drift Hamiltonian. This mirrors the standard quantum–trajectories derivation of the Lindblad equation.

Now consider that instead of ending the Trotter step with postselection, we conclude with a partial trace over the meter, as in \cref{fig:trotter-lindblad}. Then the effective state afterward is
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
    \rho(\delta) &= \mathcal{E}(\rho) = \Tr_B[U\left(\rho\otimes\ketbra{j}\right)U^\dag]\\
    &= \bra{j}U\left(\rho\otimes\ketbra{j}\right)U^\dag\ket{j}_B\\
    &\quad + \sum_{k\neq j} \expval{k}{U\left(\rho\otimes\ketbra{j}\right)U^\dag}_B\\
    &= -i\delta[H_j,\rho]-\frac{\delta}{2}\sum_{k\neq j}\{L_{kj}^\dag L_{kj},\rho\}\\
    &\quad +\delta\sum_{k\neq j}L_{kj}\rho L_{kj}^\dag +O(\delta^{3/2}) ,
\end{split}
\end{align}
which in differential form yields the Lindblad equation with system Hamiltonian $H_j$ as $\delta\rightarrow0$.

\begin{figure}[b]
    \centering
    \begin{quantikz}
\lstick{\(\ket{0}\)} & \qwbundle{} & \gate[wires=2]{e^{-iH_{AB}\sqrt{\delta}}} & \qwbundle{} & \meter{trace} \qw \\
\lstick{} & \gate{e^{-iH_A\delta}} &  & \qwbundle{} & \qw
\end{quantikz}
\caption{Effective circuit generating Lindbladian dynamics}
\label{fig:trotter-lindblad}
\end{figure}

\end{appendix}

