    Further, we provide a closed-form expression for the \gls{mse} of the \glspl{ue}' data estimator.% at the \gls{cpu}.% that estimates the .
    \item We present expressions of the \glspl{ue}' achievable rates for the proposed \gls{ota} framework. We compare them with the achievable rates obtained using a wired fronthaul and demonstrate through numerical analysis that they closely match each other.%both are comparable to each other.%the achievable rates with the proposed approach are comparable to those of the latter.
    \item We extend our proposed framework to a scenario involving imperfect \gls{csi} and explain how the performance metrics developed with perfect \gls{csi}, such as the \gls{mse} of the sufficient statistics and the \glspl{ue} data, can be adapted to situations with imperfect \gls{csi}.
    \item We compare the proposed \gls{ota} framework to a digital fronthaul scheme, offering a comprehensive analysis and discuss the trade-offs involved. We provide several insights through numerical evaluations.
