\subsection{Related work}
Let us start by defining burst errors. A nonzero vector $\be\in\F_q^n$ is called a burst of length $\fl$ if the difference between the first nonzero position $i$ and the last nonzero position $j$ of $\be$ is less than $\fl$, i.e., $j-i<\fl$. 

For a code $C\subset \F_q^n$ of length $n$ defined over a finite field $\F_q$, let $d$ be its minimum distance and $\cR=\log_q |C|/n$ be the code rate. The Singleton bound \cite{joshi58} says that
\[d-1\leq n(1-\cR).\]

\subsubsection{List decoding of burst errors}% less than minimum distance
\iffalse
Burst list decoding is a special list decoding where the errors are bursts. Given a real number $0<\rho<1$ and a positive integer $L>0$, a code $C\subset\F_q^n$ is said to be $(\rho, L)$-list-decodable if for any $\br\in\F_q^n$, there are at most $L$ codewords $\bc\in C$ such that the number of positions that $\br$ and $\bc$ differ less than $\rho n$. % or equivalently, the error vector $\br-\bc$ has Hamming weight less than $\rho n$. 
For an efficient list-decoding algorithm to exist for $C$, we need the list size $L$ as small as possible. The list-decoding capacity theorem \cite[Theorem 7.4.1]{gur19essential} provides list-decodability limits for general codes. It states that for $\rho=1-\cR-\ep$, there are rate $\cR$ codes that are $(\rho,O(1/\ep))$-list-decodable for a list-size $L$ as small as a constant $O(1/\ep)$. On the other hand, if a rate $\cR$ code is $(\rho, L)$-list decodable for $\rho=1-\cR+\ep$, then $L$ must be exponential in $n$. 

Sudan and Guruswami \cite{GSlist} showed that $\RS[n,k]$ code is $(1-\sqrt{\cR}-\ep,O(1/\ep))$-list-decodable and gave an efficient list decoding algorithm with complexity $O(n^3)$. Recently,  three seminal works ~\cite{brak23generic,guo23randomly,alr23randomly} showed that the punctured RS codes defined over random evaluation points are $(1-\cR-\ep,O(1/\ep))$-list decodable over linear size $\F_q$.  In these works, codes are proved to be list capacity-achieving without providing an efficient list-decoding algorithm. Besides, a variation of $\RS$ code, called Folded RS code, which was proved to be $(1-\cR-\ep, (1/\ep)^{1/\ep}))$-list decodable in \cite{kop23improved}. Moreover, the authors of this work also proposed an open question of whether the capacity-achieving list decodable Folded RS code can achieve a list size $O(1/\ep)$. 
\fi
 Given a real number $0<\rho<1$ and a positive integer $L>0$,
a code $C\subset\F_q^n$ is said to be $(\rho, L)$-burst list-decodable if for any received word $\br\in\F_q^n$, there are at most $L$ codewords $\bc\in C$ such that $\br-\bc$ is a burst of length $\rho n$. Generally, without constraint on burst errors, if there are at most $L$ codewords within a Hamming radius $\rho n$ from $\br$ in the adversary error model, then $C$ is called $(\rho, L)$-list decodable. For an \rRS code of length $n$ and dimension $k$, we can design the following naive burst-list decoding algorithm. Assume that for a received word $\br$, there is a burst error of length $\fl$ less than or equal to $n-k$. We erase any $n-k$ consecutive positions of $\br$ and then interpolate a polynomial of degree at most $k-1$ from the left $k$ values of $\br$. As there are at most $k+1$ such consecutive positions, the list size  $L$ is $k+1$.  As the univariate interpolation costs $\tilde{O}(n)$\footnote{The notion $\tO(n)=O(n\log^{c} n)$ for some constant integer $c>0$, which denotes quasi linear complexity.} operations \cite{von13}, the total list decoding complexity is $\tilde{O}(n^2)$. 
Roth and Vontobel \cite{roth09} showed that the list decoding radius of an $[n,k]$-linear code for burst errors is bounded by the Reiger bound, i.e., the list decoding radius obeys
\[\fl\le \frac L{L+1}(n-k),\]
where $L$ is the list size. the Reiger bound is asymptotically equal to  the Singleton bound. Let us call it the Singleton bound as well. 
Furthermore, they also showed that \rRS codes can be burst list decoded up to Singleton bound in polynomial time. 
%attain the above Reiger bound without providing efficient decoding algorithms. 
Later, Kolan and Roth \cite{kol13burst} proved that the interleaved \rRS codes (a variant of \rRS code) can be list-decoded with burst errors while attaining the Reiger bound. In \cite{Ding15}, Ding showed that a random code $C\subset \F_q^n$ is $(1-\cR-\ep, O(1/\ep))$-burst list-decodable and the random linear code is $(1-\cR-\ep, O(n))$-burst list-decodable. %Ding also showed that an $[n,k]$-cyclic code $C$ has $(1-\cR-\ep, O(n))$-burst list decodability and can be decoded in time $O(n^3)$. 
