\textbf{Keywords:} Hub Location Problems, Approximation Algorithms, Combinatorial Optimization, Network Routing, Graph Optimization

\section{Introduction}

This paper presents a hierarchy of Hub Covering Problems and provides the first approximation bounds. To structure the introduction, we first discuss the motivation for studying Hub Covering Problems, then review related work, and finally outline our contributions.

\textbf{Motivation}

We are currently experiencing a period of significant transformation. Climate change is intensifying the demand for sustainable technologies, notably electric mobility (e-mobility). High adoption of electric vehicles (EVs) has been shown to decrease net CO$_2$ emissions through 2050, even in scenarios where the complete additional capacity in the electric sector is sourced from fossil fuels by \cite{ou2021evaluating}.

Simultaneously, technological advancements have turned concepts like the Internet of Things (IoT), drones, and robots into practical realities. The integration of IoT with drones has been explored for various applications, including agriculture, inspection, smart cities, logistics delivery, and military operations, as reviewed by \cite{hajjaj2024review}. The Internet of Robotic Things (IoRT) has been applied in domains such as healthcare, smart homes, and industrial automation, enhancing the capabilities of robotic systems within these environments as shown by \cite{romeo2020internet}.

These developments present numerous challenges. Decision-makers in telecommunication, e-mobility, urban planning, and cargo logistics must address complex problems involving both location and routing decisions. For instance, \cite{santosh2023drones} suggest using drones to enable the integration of IoT applications in smart cities making optimal deployment strategies to ensure effective coverage and connectivity fundamental.  Similarly, the widespread adoption of EVs requires strategic planning for charging infrastructure placement to support efficient routing and accessibility as described by \cite{zaino2024electric}.


In the domain of \textbf{urban planning}, diverse challenges arise. For instance, transit hubs and public transport stations must be placed to minimize overall travel times. Planning principles are illustrated by \cite{mcleod2017urban}. Similarly, the strategic siting of critical infrastructure, such as hospitals or fire stations, is driven by the requirement that every residence remains within a predefined travel time threshold. This framework also extends to the placement of Automated External Defibrillators (AEDs) within buildings, where rapid accessibility is essential. A more detailed description on siting of critical infrastructure can be found by \cite{maliszewski2010spatial} and \cite{farahani2019or}. 

The complexity of location and routing problems further increases for \textbf{cargo logistics}. In this domain, merely ensuring that customers have quick access to a nearby consolidation point is insufficient; comprehensive multi-stop tours, goods transfers, and modal shifts must also be considered. The classical hub-and-spoke network model, detailed in \cite{farahani2013hub} and \cite{o1994hub}, remains a cornerstone in addressing these challenges. Similar issues are evident in airline and train transport systems, where the aggregation of goods can reduce costs but simultaneously complicate logistical planning.

The field of \textbf{telecommunication} faces analogous challenges. In ensuring ubiquitous connectivity, through WiFi (essential for IoT), 5G, or the emerging 6G standards as discussed in \cite{10461409}, network designers must decide the placement of base stations depending on the signal strengths. Works such as \cite{li2020load} highlight the challenge of designing wireless metropolitan area networks, where both spatial coverage and load distribution are critical.

\textbf{E-mobility} is rapidly gaining importance as societies strive to address climate change. Here, the deployment of electric vehicle charging stations (as well as charging infrastructures for robots and drones) demands careful planning. The limited range of electric vehicles necessitates that charging hubs are positioned such that all areas lie within a feasible driving distance, as explored in \cite{garau2024methodology} and \cite{unterluggauer2022electric}.

\textbf{Related Work}

All of these applications have threshold constraints such as limiting the maximum travel time to an AED, the signal strength in a wireless network, or the limited range of an electric vehicle in common. Traditional network design models often focus solely on minimizing summed edge lengths or summed travel distances (cf. \cite{yang1998models}). \textbf{Hub Location Problems} (HLP) have emerged as a versatile mathematical framework that has been successfully applied in various sectors, including cargo delivery, telecommunication, postal, airline, and ground transport networks (see, e.g., \cite{klincewicz1998Hub} and \cite{alumur2008network} for comprehensive surveys).

In the HLP, the goal is to open hubs and establish a routing system based on these. The objective is to minimize travel distances and/or reduce hub opening costs. A special case, where the opening costs of any hub is $1$ is called \textbf{unweighted} HLP.  
For some HLP, approximation algorithms have been developed. For instance for the $p$-Hub Median Problem and the uncapacitated HLP, \cite{benedito2019approximation} gave a first bound by reducing it to the Facility Location Problem. This bound was partially improved by \cite{jost2023approximation}. Hardness results for various HLP have been shown by \cite{wang2021improved}.   

\textbf{Hub Covering Problems} (HCP) are a subset of HLP and were initially defined by \cite{campbell1994integer}. They are characterized by additional threshold constraints. Depending on the application, these constraints may apply to individual edges or to complete tours, resulting in multiple variants. In line with \cite{campbell1994integer}, we consider three different variants of HCP restricting distances of a tour (variant 1), an edge (variant 2) or branch-to-hub connections (variant 3). Each of these variants exists in both a \textbf{single allocation} (SA) and \textbf{multi allocation} (MA) version. In SA, branches are restricted to being connected to only one hub, while in MA, any tour can be planned individually. Additionally, the capacitated variant, which restricts the number of open hubs, is briefly discussed, as it is primarily considered in the context of HLP. To distinguish the different versions, we refer to them using \textit{SA} or \textit{MA} followed by the variant number (e.g., MA~2 denotes the multi allocation version of the second variant). For SA~1 \cite{calik2009tabu} proposed a tabu-search. For SA~1 and MA~1 \cite{karimi2011Hub} developed a heuristic and applied it to the Turkish postal network and Iranian hub airports. We show that SA~3 and MA~3 are equivalent to the weighted Set Cover problem. The link between Set Cover and Location problem was firstly drawn by \cite{toregas1971location}, introducing the location set covering problem. 

HCP are closely related to \textbf{$p$-Hub Center Problems}, where the maximal tour length or length of a used edge is minimized. The additional constraint for the HCP form the objectives of the corresponding $p$-Hub Center Problems. While several special cases of the $p$-Hub Center Problems are known to be polynomially solvable (see \cite{campbell2007p}), and an approximation algorithm has been proposed by \cite{jost2023approximation2}, these approaches cannot be directly transferred to HCP. This is, because a suboptimal increase in the tour length is part of the approximation bound in $p$-Hub Center Problems, but give an invalid solution for HCP. Hence, no approximation guarantee has been established for HCP thus far.

\textbf{Contributions}

With this paper, we show for any variant either the non-existence of an approximation bound or provides lower and upper bounds on its approximation guarantee. Moreover, a hierarchy of the problems is presented, showing that some variants are a special case of another variant. In Figure \ref{full2} a complete hierarchy of the considered HCP is visualized. Arrows show that a variant is a special case from another. For instance an arrow from SA~1 to SA~2 is equivalent to SA~2 $\leq_p$ SA~1, where $\leq_p$ denotes the existence of a polynomial-time reduction. In other words, if $A\leq_p B$, then $A$ is a special case of $B$ implying that the approximation bound of $A$ cannot exceed the approximation bound of $B$. 

