This contribution can be summarized in following points:
\begin{itemize}
    \item A hierarchy of the problem variants is derived:\newline MA~3 $\leq_p$ MA~2 $\leq_p$ MA~1 and \newline SA~3 $\leq_p$ SA~2 $\leq_p$ SA~1.
    \item SA/MA~3 $\in \Theta(\log(|B|)$ providing lower bounds for any variant.\newline This still holds even if all opening costs are set to $1$.  
    \item A $|B|^2$ approximation algorithm for MA~1 and MA~2 is presented.
    \item There exists no approximation bound for SA~1 and SA~2.
    \item There exists no approximation bound for any capacitated HCP.
\end{itemize}

A brute force approach to give additionally an $\frac{|H|}{k}$ bound for any constant $k$ is shown for the unweighted version of MA~1.




The structure of this paper is as follows:
In Section \ref{chap pd}, the HCP considered in this paper are formally defined. Each variant is illustrated with a corresponding practical application to illustrate the difference between the variants and to demonstrate its relevance to real-world scenarios. Section \ref{chap p} structures the results into six lemmas, which are subsequently proven. Finally, we present the conclusion in Section \ref{chap c}.  


\section{Model Formulation}\label{chap pd}

%The models in this chapter follow the problem variants defined by \cite{campbell1994integer} and the notation as in \cite{jost2023approximation}.

HCP are defined on metric graphs $G=(V,E)$. The vertices consist of \textbf{branches} and \textbf{hubs}. Each hub has a \textbf{setup cost}. The edge weights represent a non-negative \textbf{distance function}. If an application allows a branch to be upgraded to a hub, this is modeled by adding an additional hub vertex with distance $0$ to the corresponding branch. Additionally, a set of \textbf{delivery tasks} is given, which must be fulfilled. Each delivery task is a tuple of branches indicating a starting branch and an end branch. A task must be served by creating a tour with the corresponding start and end branch. This tour has one or two hubs as stopovers. Depending on the application, multiple people or goods can be consolidated and transported together at the hubs, yielding cost savings. This is modeled by a given \textbf{discount factor} $\alpha\in \{0,1\}$ reducing the hub-to-hub connection distance. 

In HCP the challenge is, that certain connections or tours are not valid, because their distance exceeds a certain \textbf{threshold} $\phi$. Different variants of the HCP consider different types of distances (e.g. tours and connections) which must not exceed $\phi$. These variants are further explained in the corresponding subsections.  

To summarize, the input of the problem is:

\begin{itemize}
	\item $\mathcal{B}\subset V$: a finite \textbf{set of branches}
	\item $\mathcal{H}\subset V$: a finite \textbf{set of potential hubs}
        \item $c_{h_1},c_{h_2},...,c_{h_{|\mathcal{H}|}}\in \mathbb{R}^+$: \textbf{setup costs} of the hubs
	\item $d_{i,j}\in \mathbb{R}^+$: a non-negative \textbf{distance function} for any $i,j\in V$
	\item $\tau\subseteq \mathcal{B} \times\mathcal{B}$: a \textbf{set of delivery tasks}
    \item $\alpha\in \{0,1\}$: a \textbf{discount factor} for hub-to-hub connections
    \item $\phi \in \mathbb{R}^+$: a \textbf{threshold for distances} of used connections or tours
\end{itemize}
In addition, the following decision variables are used:
\begin{itemize}
	\item $Y_h\in\{0,1 \}$: indicates if hub $h$ is \textbf{opened} for $h\in \mathcal{H}$
	\item $X_{bhh'b'}\in\{0,1 \}$: indicates if the corresponding \textbf{tour} is applied for $b,b'\in\mathcal{B}$, $h,h'\in\mathcal{H}$
\end{itemize}

In HCP, it must be decided which hubs to open (by decision variable $Y$). Based on this decision, tours are created (by decision variable $X$). The hub decisions must ensure that this is possible for any delivery task. Any length of a tour is a constraint and not part of the objective function, which focuses on opening hubs at the lowest possible cost. 



Since all variants share the same objective and most constraints, we first introduce the general framework before detailing the specific constraints of each variant.

The mathematical model is as follows:

\begin{align}
		\label{ZF}\min  &~\sum_{h\in \mathcal{H}}Y_h\cdot c_h\\
		\label{med3}&\sum_{h\in \mathcal{H}}\sum_{h'\in \mathcal{H}}X_{bhh'b'}\geq 1&&\forall (b,b')\in \tau\\
		\label{med4}&X_{bhh'b'}\leq Y_h  && \forall b,b'\in \mathcal{B}\quad \forall h,h'\in \mathcal{H}\\
		\label{med5}&X_{bhh'b'}\leq Y_{h'}&& \forall b,b'\in \mathcal{B}\quad \forall h,h'\in \mathcal{H}\\	
        %&(d_{b,h}+\alpha \cdot d_{hh'}+d_{h'b'})\cdot X_{bhh'b'}\leq \phi&& \forall b,b'\in \mathcal{B}; \forall h,h'\in \mathcal{H}\\
		\label{med1}&Y_h\in \{0,1\} && \forall h\in \mathcal{H}\\
		\label{med2}&X_{bhh'b'}\in \{0,1\} && \forall b,b'\in \mathcal{B}\quad \forall h,h'\in \mathcal{H}
	\end{align}

The objective \ref{ZF} minimizes the total opening cost of the hubs. Constraint \ref{med3} ensures that a tour is selected for each delivery task. Constraints \ref{med4} and \ref{med5} guarantee that only open hubs are used within these tours. Lastly, Constraints \ref{med1} and \ref{med2} define that hubs are either open or closed and tours are either utilized or not. Additionally, tours involving only a single hub are represented by $X_{bhhb'}$. Given that $d$ forms a metric, it holds that $d_{hh}=0$.

The presented framework applies to the  multi allocation version. In contrast, the single allocation version assigns branches to hubs instead of tours. This implies that any tour starting (or ending) in branch $b$ shares the same first (or second) hub. Consequently, the formulation focuses on branch-to-hub connections, requiring a reformulation of $X$. We assume that every branch is part of at least one tour. The following formulas present the multi allocation formulation on the left and the corresponding single allocation formulation on the right. To reduce notation complexity, quantifiers are omitted.


