Since any MA solution is valid for SA, it follows directly that MA $i$ $\leq_p$ SA $i$ for the same variant $i\in\{1,2,3\}$. Since $d$ is not yet utilized, the optimal solution is to only open the cheapest hub and use it for all delivery tasks. Therefore, additional constraints are incorporated into the variants, motivated
by real-world applications. The specification of exemplary applications should help to understand each problem variant. 

\textbf{Variant 1}

\textit{$\phi$ restricts the distance of a valid tour.}

\textit{Exemplary applications:}

%Since this variant restricts the distance of a tour, it is applicable if every tour needs to be performed in a certain amount of time. For instance, consider shipping promises such as ``delivery within 24-hours''.

\begin{itemize}
    \item Network design for maximal shipping time promises (e.g. same-day-delivery)
    %\item Network design for shipping promises such as ``delivery within 24-hours''.
\end{itemize}

\textit{Additional Constraint:}

Multi allocation:
\[ (d_{bh}+\alpha \cdot d_{hh'}+d_{h'b'})\cdot X_{bhh'b'}\leq \phi\quad \forall b,b'\in \mathcal{B}\quad \forall h,h'\in \mathcal{H}\]

Single allocation:
\[ (d_{bh}+\alpha \cdot d_{hh'}+d_{h'b'})\cdot X_{bh}\cdot X_{bh'}\leq \phi\quad \forall b,b'\in \mathcal{B}\quad \forall h,h'\in \mathcal{H}\]
\textbf{Variant 2}

\textit{$\phi$ restricts the distance of a valid connection.}

In this variant, the distance function is only necessary to decide, if a connection is available for selection or not. Therefore, in order to avoid as much notation as possible, variant 2 will be considered on unweighted, not fully-connected graphs.

\textit{Exemplary application:}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Integrated Airport Location and Airline Network Planning, such that customers do not need to switch airplanes often, while considering maximal flight distances of airplanes.
    \item Network design while considering labor protection laws, such that drivers are only allowed driving a maximal time before taking a break.
    \item Network design for electric fleets, which can only drive a maximal distance before recharge.
\end{itemize}


\textit{Additional Constraints:}

Multi allocation:
\[ d_{bh}\cdot X_{bhh'b'}\leq \phi\quad \forall b,b'\in \mathcal{B}\quad \forall h,h'\in \mathcal{H}\]

\[ \alpha d_{hh'}\cdot X_{bhh'b'}\leq \phi\quad \forall b,b'\in \mathcal{B}\quad \forall h,h'\in \mathcal{H}\]

\[ d_{h'b}\cdot X_{bhh'b'}\leq \phi\quad \forall b,b'\in \mathcal{B}\quad \forall h,h'\in \mathcal{H}\]

Single allocation:
\[ d_{bh}\cdot X_{bh}\leq \phi\quad \forall b\in \mathcal{B}\quad \forall h\in \mathcal{H}\]

\[ \alpha d_{hh'}\cdot X_{bh}\cdot X_{b'h'}\leq \phi\quad \forall b,b'\in \mathcal{B}\quad \forall h,h'\in \mathcal{H}\]


\textbf{Variant 3}

\textit{$\phi$ restricts the distance of a valid branch-to-hub connection. Branches only need to be connected to one hub and tasks are not considered.}

Similarly to variant 2, in order to avoid as much notation as possible, variant 3 will be considered on unweighted, bipartite graphs. The difference between SA~3 and MA~3 is, that in MA~3 one branch might be connected to multiple hubs. However, it would still be valid to connect the branch to one of these hubs as in single allocation. Since MA can not improve the SA solution and the SA solution is valid for MA, this variant is only considered in one version we call SA/MA~3. 

