\begin{lem}\label{lem2}
    MA~1 can be $|B|^2$ approximated. In the unweighted case, it can additionally be $\frac{|H|}{k}$ approximated for any constant $k$.
\end{lem}


%\subsection{MA~1 can be $|B|^2$ approximated. In the special case, where any Hub has equal opening costs, it can additionally be $\frac{|H|}{k})$ approximated for any constant $k$.}


Both claims are shown individually.

\textbf{Proof of $|B|^2$}: This bound can be achieved by opening hubs for any delivery task individually. Consider task $\tau_i\in \tau$. If this would be the only task, the problem could be solved optimally by testing any of the $H^2$ possible combinations for a first and second hub. Let $cost(\tau_i)$ be these opening costs. Since only $|\tau|\leq |B|^2$ tasks exist, by solving any task individually the claim follows:
\[ \sum_{\tau_i \in \tau}cost(\tau_i)\leq \sum_{\tau_i \in \tau}OPT\leq |B^2|\cdot OPT\] \qed


\textbf{Proof of $\frac{|H|}{k}$}: In this bound the unweighted case is considered where any hub has opening cost of $1$. There exists at most $|H|$ potential solutions which only open one hub and therefore have an objective value of $1$. Analogously, there exist at most \[\sum_{i=1}^k \binom{|H|}{i}\] potential solutions with objective at most $k$. Since $k$ is constant, this term grows polynomially with respect to $|H|$ and it is possible to test any potential solution with at most $k$ open hubs in polynomial time. If no solution is found, any potential hub can be opened, yielding to a $k$ objective solution (or showing that no solution exists). We now do a case distinction on the optimal objective OPT.

1) OPT$\leq k$: If the optimal solution uses at most $k$ hubs, it is found by this procedure.

2) OPT$>k$: In this case, the procedure gives a solution with an objective value of $|H|$, while OPT$>k$. This directly gives a $\frac{|H|}{k+1}$ approximation factor.\qed  


\begin{lem}\label{lem4}
    SA~2 is a special case of SA~1 and MA~2 is a special case of MA~1.
\end{lem}

%\subsection{SA/MA~2 is a special case of SA/MA~1}

\textbf{Proof:} Since the proofs are equivalent, we write SA/MA instead of separating both claims. We construct a SA/MA~1 instance which is equivalent to a given SA/MA~2 instance. To achieve this, we demonstrate that any valid solution in SA/MA~2 is still valid in the SA/MA~1 construction and any non valid solution in SA/MA~2 is also non valid in SA/MA~1. Furthermore, since the construction does not change any opening costs, the objective of any SA/MA~2 solution is the same for the SA/MA~1 construction. 

The set of branches, hubs, opening costs and delivery tasks can directly be transferred. Since an instance for SA/MA~2 does not contain weights, they need to be added such that the graph is metric. Therefore, we add weights of $1$ for any existing edge in SA/MA~2 and $2$ otherwise. Furthermore, we set $\alpha=0.5$ and $\phi=2.75$. Since only edges of weight $1$ and $2$ exist, the triangle inequality holds and the distances form a metric.

To sum up, in the following way an SA/MA~1 instance can be derived from a given SA/MA~2 one:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The branches, hubs, opening costs and delivery tasks are applied as in SA/MA~2.
    \item Edges of weight $1$ are included for any edge existing in SA/MA~2.
    \item Edges of weight $2$ are included for any edge not existing in SA/MA~2.
    \item The discount factor is set to $\alpha=0.5$ and the threshold is set to $\phi=2.75$.
\end{itemize}

For simplicity, consider an SA/MA~2 instance as in Figure \ref{figure7}. An SA/MA~1 instance is created by a fully connected graph with the same vertices. The edge weights are set to $1$ for any edge already existing in SA/MA~2 and $2$ else. In Figure \ref{figure8} the edges are added. Black edges have weight of $1$, while blue edges have weight of $2$. 

