To show that SA/MA~1 on the constructed instance is equivalent to SA/MA~2 it is left to show that the cost of any valid path (Figure \ref{ex1}, \ref{ex3}) does not exceed $\phi=2.75$ and any invalid path (Figure \ref{ex2}, \ref{ex4}) does. This can be easily calculated by bounding the path length $P_1,P_2,P_3$ and $P_4$.

\begin{itemize}
    \item (1): $P_1=1+1=2<2.75$ 
    \item (2): $P_2=1+0.5\cdot 1+1=2.5<2.75$  
    \item (3): $P_3\geq1+2=3>2.75$
    \item (4): $P_4\geq1+0.5\cdot 2+1=3>2.75$
\end{itemize}

Hence, both problem statements are equivalent.

Notice that there exist SA/MA~1 instances which can not be transformed to a SA/MA~2 instance. Hence, SA/MA~2 is a special case.\qed

\begin{lem}\label{lem3}
    SA~2 can not be approximated in polynomial time unless $P=NP$.
\end{lem}


%\subsection{SA~2 can not be approximated in polynomial time unless $P=NP$}

\textbf{Proof:} We show this by reducing SA~2 to the $n$-Queens Completion Problem, which is an extension of the classic $n$-Queens Problem. In that problem, a $n\times n$ chessboard is given. The task is to find an arrangement to place $n$ queens on the board which do not attack each other. This means no two queens can be in the same row, column or diagonal. In the $n$-Queens Completion Problem, some of the $n$ queens are already on the board and therefore already part of the solution. This problem was first introduced in 1850. More recently, \cite{glock2022n} showed, that a valid solution always exists if at most $\frac{n}{60}$ queens are placed. Moreover, \cite{gent2017complexity} have shown that it is NP-complete. 

For a given $n$-Queens completion instance we will construct an SA~2 instance and show that a solution of the SA~2 instance would solve $n$-Queens completion. Since $n$-Queens completion is NP-hard, this is not possible in polynomial time unless $P=NP$.   

Since two queens would attack each other if they are in the same row, we label the queens $Q_1,Q_2,...,Q_n$ corresponding to the row they are/will be placed. Likewise we label the branches $B_1,B_2,...,B_n$ which represent the queen of the corresponding row. Each square $S_{1,1},...,S_{n,n}$ is represented by a potential hub $H_{1,1},...,H_{n,n}$. If hub $H_{i,j}$ is opened, a queen is placed on $S_{i,j}$. A connection between two hubs represents, that the corresponding squares are neither in the same column, row or diagonal (corresponding queens would not attack each other). Since any pair of queens should not attack each other, a delivery task is established for every pair of branches. 

For a given $n$-Queens completion instance we construct a corresponding HCP in the following way:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Establish $n$ Branches, representing the queen on each row.
    \item Establish $n^2$ potential hubs, representing each square of the board.
    \item Establish $n^2$ tasks, one between each branch representing that the corresponding queens don't attack each other.
    \item Establish hub-to-hub connections for any two hubs $H_{i,j}$ and $H_{k,l}$ with $i\neq k$, $j\neq l$ and $|i-j|\neq|k-l|$ (representing any squares which not attack each other).
    \item Establish branch-to-hub connection (e.g. $B_j$ to $H_{i,j})$ for already fixed queens ($Q_j$ is on Square $S_{i,j}$).
    \item Establish a branch-to-hub connection for not fixed queens $Q_j$ to any hub representing a square of this row ($H_{1,j},...,H_{n,j}$). 
\end{itemize}

For instance consider an instance for the $3$-Queens completion problem as in Figure \ref{fig:n-queens-example}. For simplicity we will use chess-notation for the column index (e.g. $a$ instead of $1$).

