\begin{lem}\label{lem1}
    SA/MA~3 is in $\Theta(\log|B|)$ even for $c_{H}=1$.
\end{lem}


%\subsection{SA/MA~3 is in $\Theta(\log|B|)$ even for $c_{H}=1$}

\textbf{Proof:} We show that SA/MA~3 is equivalent to the weighted Set Cover Problem and, if all opening costs are 1, it is equivalent to the Set Cover Problem. 

Since \cite{lund1994hardness} established that Set Cover has an approximation lower bound of $\Omega(\log|E|)$, and \cite{chvatal1979greedy} introduced a $O(\log|E|)$ greedy-algorithm for weighted Set Cover, it follows that the same approximation bounds apply to SA/MA~3. To complete the proof, we must demonstrate the equivalence of these problems.

In the weighted Set Cover Problem, we are given a set of elements and a collection of sets, where each set has a positive weight and covers at least one element. The goal is to select a subset of sets such that all elements are covered at least once while minimizing the total weight. If all sets have weight of 1, the problem simplifies to the unweighted Set Cover Problem.

To establish the equivalence, we construct a corresponding SA/MA~3 instance from any given (weighted) Set Cover instance. In this transformation, elements correspond to branches, and sets correspond to hubs. Specifically, for each set $S_i$ covering element $E_j$, we define a hub $H_i$ connected to a branch $B_j$. The opening costs of $H_i$ are set to the weight of $S_i$. The transformation follows these steps:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Create a branch $B_j$ for each element $E_j$.
    \item Create a hub $H_i$ for each set $S_i$.
    \item Assign the opening cost $c_i$ of $H_i$ to be the weight of $S_i$.
    \item Connect a branch $B_j$ to a hub $H_i$ if and only if $S_i$ covers $E_j$.
\end{itemize}

Notice that a transferred unweighted Set Cover Problem gives opening costs of $1$ for any hub. 
This transformation can also be reversed: a given SA/MA~3 instance can be converted into an equivalent Set Cover instance, as illustrated by an example in Figure \ref{xxx}. 

