\begin{lem}\label{lem5}
    Capacitated SA/MA~3 is not solvable in polynomial time.
\end{lem}

%\subsection{Capacitated SA/MA~3 is not solvable in polynomial time}

\textbf{Proof:} Assume, for contradiction, that the lemma is false. Then, for any given unweighted SA/MA~3 instance with an optimal objective value of OPT, it would be possible to execute an approximation algorithm under any restriction on the number of hubs $p=1,2,...,|H|$ once. If $p<$OPT, the algorithm must indicate that no valid solution exists. When $p=$OPT, the algorithm produces a feasible solution, which must also be optimal since the objective value is bounded by $p$. Consequently, applying an approximation algorithm for the capacitated SA/MA~3 problem would yield an exact solution for SA/MA~3.

This contradicts lemma \ref{lem1} that SA/MA 3 can not be approximated better than $\Theta(log|B|)$. Consequently, no approximation algorithm for capacitated SA/MA~3 exists.\qed

\section{Conclusion}\label{chap c}

HCP provide a framework for designing logistics networks by integrating the well-established HLP model with distance constraints, which are crucial for applications such as urban planning, telecommunication, cargo logistics and e-mobility. 

With this paper we give the first approximation bounds for these problems. It was shown that SA/MA~3 is equivalent to (weighted) set cover. Furthermore, it is proven that SA~1, SA~2, and capacitated variants do not admit any approximation bound, implying that large instances must be solved heuristically. Additionally, the problem hierarchies MA~3$\leq_p$ MA~2$\leq_p$ MA~1 and SA~3$\leq_p$ SA~2$\leq_p$ SA~1 are established, and a first approximation bound for MA~1 is provided. However, a significant gap remains between the lower bound of $\Omega(\log(|B|))$ and the upper bound of $|B|^2$ (or $\frac{|H|}{k}$ in the unweighted case), which should be narrowed in future research.

Due to the connection between weighted set cover and HCP a logical next step is to apply Set Cover heuristics as the greedy algorithm from \cite{chvatal1979greedy} to HCP. However, applying it to MA~1 is challenging, as a tour may consist of a pair of hubs and covering a pair of hubs may reduce the cost of a different pair as they might share one hub. Promising heuristic approaches could leverage techniques such as randomization and MIP relaxation. For instance, allowing $Y$ to take non-binary values provides a lower bound for any instance, and prioritizing hubs $h$ with high $Y_h$ values is an intuitive strategy.

